  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV2">
      <l>mutalkaru vaṇainta vuripporu ḷakamē.<anchor xml:id="akam"/></l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
  <l><foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akam</foreign> is the suitable poetic content<note xml:lang="en" place="foot"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">uripporuḷ</hi></q> is a difficult term to translate. I have intentionally provided a literal and broad translation, in keeping with how I have translated <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ</hi></term> in other places. In practice, it refers to the dominant emotional theme in each setting. These are enumerated in <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> 13.</note> that is joined<note xml:lang="en" place="foot"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">aṇainta</hi></q> can be interpreted in a number of different ways here, both syntactically and semantically. I have chosen this translation as I believe it most closely mirrors the definition given in the TP, upon which I think this is based, where the phrase <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṟai ciṟantaṉavē</hi></q> implies <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">uripporuḷ</hi></q> has a greater level of importance. Thus here I have centred it in my translation.</note> to the basic elements<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">There are other ways to translate <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mutal</hi></q>. Here, it is understood as a basis in which the other elements exist, hence ‘basic’. The other way to translate would be ‘first’. The justification for this is found in an interpretation of TP 3i (cited in the apparatus to this <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign>), where <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mutal</hi></q> is so called because it is the first way one can identify the poetic setting when <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mutal</hi></q>, <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karu</hi></q>, and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">uri</hi></q> are present, at least according to Iḷampūraṇar.</note> and the constituent elements.<note xml:lang="en" place="foot"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karu</hi></q> has the literal sense of an ‘embryo’ or ‘germ’. The <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karupporuḷ</hi></q> are the gods, people, flora, and fauna proper to each <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">tiṇai</hi></term>. As with my renderings of <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mutal</hi></q> and <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">uri</hi></q>, I have prioritised a functional translation over an etymologically faithful one. ‘Embryonic elements’ would render <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karu</hi></q> literally, but in English ‘embryonic’ connotes something is undeveloped. ‘Germinal’ (favoured by Selby) is perhaps the most accurate single rendering, but it has a similar problem to ‘embryonic’. ‘Native elements’ (preferred by Zvelebil) captures that the <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karu</hi></q> belong to and arise within the setting, but it describes their origin rather than their function within the poetic system, and is not etymologically exact. Buck and Paramasivam’s ‘Matrix Aspect’ preserves something of the generative sense of <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karu</hi></q>, but emphasises the creative aspect, which, if anything, seems more appropriate for <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mutal</hi></q>. Without a satisfactory rendering that is also etymologically close, I have gone with ‘constituent elements’, which I think at least conveys something of their functionality.</note></l>
</lg>
      </div>
</body>
</text>
<text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV2comm1">eṉpatu collap paṭṭaṉa vuḷ akap poruḷ ām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.</p>
<p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the way in which the poetic content of the interior genre<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">This is an awkward idea to render in English. It refers to the genre of poetry that relates to the interior emotional world. In practice, this refers to poetry that concerns different facets of romantic relationships.</note> occurs amongst those that have been mentioned.<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">This is referring back to the three categories mentioned in <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> 1 – <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">akapporuḷ</hi></q>, <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">puṟapporuḷ</hi></q>, and what the commentary identifies as <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">akappuṟapporuḷ</hi></q>.</note></p>
</div>
<div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV2comm2">mutaluṅ karuvuñ cārnta vurip poruḷ akap poruḷ ām eṉṟa vāṟu.</p>  
<p xml:lang="en"><term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">akapporuḷ</hi></term> is the suitable poetic content that is attached to the basic elements and the constituent elements.</p>
</div>
<div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV2comm3">mutal eṉap paṭṭatiṭamum kālamum eṉṟuṇarvatu.<anchor xml:id="mutal"/> karu veṉap paṭṭa poruṭ ceykai yoḻittuk koḷka. 
vatuvum uriyum paṇpeṉap paṭum. innāṉkiṉ kūṭṭamun tiṇai yeṉap paṭum eṉpatu. <num>2.</num></p>
<p xml:lang="en">The thing called <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mutal</hi></term> is understood as 
place and time. Understand the poetic content called <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karu</hi></term> excludes the [human] action.<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">This is slightly ambiguous to me. My best guess is that 
<q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">ceykai</hi></q> here refers to the human action taking place in the poem – the hero 
going off to work, for example – and that the content being referred to is the material components of the poetic 
setting, i.e., the flora and fauna.</note> That and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">uri</hi></term> are called 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">paṇpu</hi></term><note xml:lang="en" place="foot">I understand this as 
‘natural property’ and the referent of <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">atu</hi></q> is 
<q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karu</hi></q>. This would then be saying that the constituent elements 
and emotional themes in a poem are natural to the basic elements – the time and place. In other words, certain 
poetic settings have specific properties and themes in their nature.</note> and the group of those 
four<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">Presumably this is referring to <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">iṭam</hi></term>, 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kālam</hi></term>, <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">karu</hi></term>, and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">uri</hi></term>.</note> is called <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">tiṇai</hi></term>.</p>
</div>
</body></text><standOff corresp="#TNV2" type="wordsplit">
