<standOff type="notes1" corresp="#TNV2"><note xml:lang="ta" target="#akam"><term xml:lang="en">Cf. TP3n.1–2:</term> mutalkaru vuripporu ḷeṉṟa mūṉṟē | nuvaluṅ kālai muṟaiciṟan taṉavē</note></standOff>
<standOff type="notes4" corresp="#TNV2"><note xml:lang="en" target="#akam">Every <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign> in the TNV, except for TNV 7, explicitly states the <foreign xml:lang="la">definiendum</foreign>. I see no reason why that should not be the case here. There is no sensible option for a term to be supplied from the previous <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign>, and the commentary clearly states that the topic of the <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign> is defining <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akapporuḷ</foreign>, strongly suggesting that the correct reading is indeed <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">akamē</hi></q>. The <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign>-s of the TNV do not consistently end in the particle <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">-ē</foreign>, so one could argue it is unnecessary, but all of the surrounding <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign>-s have it, and all but one of the first fourteen do as well (the point at which the TNV moves on from describing the <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">tiṇai</foreign> system to poetic situations). In the case of TNV 7, I speculate that it was unnecessary to name the <foreign xml:lang="la">definiendum</foreign> as it was already established that the different <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">karu</foreign> were being enumerated in that string of <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign>-s, and it would have been obvious that that specific <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cūttiram</foreign> was naming the gods.</note></standOff>
